ds of psa that is global  alignment and local alignment  on the other hand multiple sequence  alignment is the method used to compare  multiple long and short sequences to  find matching portions introduce gaps  and find consensus sequence  here in the two  pictures  which we can see on the slide  tells us about the different r codes for  psa and msa respectively  first we need to load the libraries then  we need to  load the data string and then  we need to give a command for pairwise  alignment or multiple sequence alignment  accordingly and then the last command is  for the print  printing the alignment  genome sequencing there are mainly three  genome sequencing which we which i have  learned  so the first one is targeted ngs  sequencing it is a rapid and cost  effective way to detect known and normal  variants in selected sets of genes or  genomic regions whole genome sequencing  provides a comprehensive picture of  information in the genome by reading  both coding and non-coding sequences in  short it reads the whole genome from  start to the end involving the  non-coding regions and the coding  regions wgs generates more detailed look  at the information of our dna  whole exome sequencing on other hand  targets only the protein coding regions  of the genome providing less coverage  and is it all only gives us the  information about the coding regions of  the genome  variant calling refers to the task of  identifying possible variations in  genome or transcriptome sequence with  respect to chosen reference sequence so  it tells us about the  different variations in the genome  with respect to the reference you know  reference sequence which helps us to  identify the mutation  in the reference genome  according to the reference genome in the  sample so there are two types of variant  variations variant callings that is  germline variant calling and somatic  variant calling so jump line variant  callings are these are the variations  which are present by birth and does not  get transferred from one generation to  another and are usually taken as the  reference sequence of the standard  species  for this species of interest somatic  variant calling on the other hand are  the variations which are developed with  the course of time and does not get  transferred from one generation to  another and they are usually taken as  control somatic sample cell samples  the whole genome  is read  into two methods two methods that is by  period and reads and by single entry so  in pair and reads the fragment is right  from both the ends towards the center of  the fragment in single end the  sequencing fragment is read from one end  to the other towards the another end  here is phylogenetic tree which we can  see it's a dendogram there are many  different uh tree representations in  phylogenetic analysis but here we have  taken the dendogram so phylogenetic tree  tells us about the closely evolutionary  relationship with among the species and  their origin  so here in the diagram a b c d e are  referred to as texon and the branch  which is emerging from a common origin  which is the node  gives two clade that is c and d and a  and b so c and d is in one plate  which tells us that they are very  closely  related to each other we they are very  if they are in close relationship with  each other  mutability in simple terms describe the  observed rate of given position to the  change or muted  differential mutation is an evolutionary  algorithm that processes a population of  individual represented by dimensional  vectors of real numbers  copy number variation is a very is this  type of variation in which a section of  genome is repeated and the number of  repeats in the genome varies between  individual in this human  population  here are the different pipeline graphs  which i have learned during my course  to  to identify  different  things like variant calling mutability  analysis phylogenetic tree analysis and  copy number variation so di